# Verifying a dispatched subagent's work, not just its self-report

## Why this exists

C413 (16 Aug 2026): three parallel backend subagents were dispatched to rework trigger-point
wiring, each instructed to run the full regression suite (`pytest tests/services/ tests/api/ -q`,
~7 minutes) as its own closing evidence. Two of the three stalled the same way: the subagent
backgrounded that long run, its own turn ended before the background process resolved, and its
final report was "waiting for the monitor" — no pass/fail counts, no completion-log entry, no
usable evidence — repeated 2-3 times per agent even after being told directly to stop waiting and
run things synchronously. Each stall cost a full round-trip (the orchestrating session had to
notice, redirect, and wait again) on top of the ~200-900K tokens and 15+ minutes the agent had
already spent on genuinely correct, finished work sitting behind that stalled report.

## The fix that actually worked

**Stop waiting on the subagent's self-report and verify directly**, rather than sending repeated
follow-ups:

1. `git status --short -- <the files this task was scoped to touch>` — confirms real edits
   happened and roughly what.
2. Re-run the task's own cited test file(s) directly, yourself, in the foreground:
   `pytest <the specific test file the task brief named> -q`.
3. Re-run the task's named regression guard (a smaller, targeted suite — e.g. the one existing
   test file the task must not break), not the full 7-minute suite, unless you specifically need
   that number too.
4. If both come back green, treat the task as done — write the completion-log entry yourself
   citing the commands you personally just ran, rather than waiting indefinitely for the
   subagent to eventually produce its own.

This was faster and more reliable than continued follow-up messages in both cases it was used,
and produces the same class of evidence (`openspec-tdd-mandate.md`'s "real command/test evidence"
requirement) regardless of which side actually ran the command.

## A second failure mode: a confident report that got ahead of reality

C425 (22 Aug 2026): a subagent implementing Phase 1 wrote its own `tasks.md` completion-log entry
— *"Phase 1 code-complete, **committed locally to `dev`**"* — while the files were still
uncommitted and it was, by its own later admission, still waiting on the coverage run it needed
before actually running the commit. This is the inverse of the stalled-report problem above: not
"no evidence, still waiting," but **confident, specific, plausible-sounding evidence that describes
a future action as already done.** The orchestrating session almost relayed "committed" to the
human on the strength of that log entry alone. The same pattern showed up smaller-scale in C424:
a subagent updated `tasks.md`'s own `status:` frontmatter to `partial` but never touched
`proposal.md`'s — which is the actual status source of truth per `openspec-verify`'s routing table
— leaving the two files silently disagreeing.

**The check that catches this:** before relaying any subagent's "done"/"committed"/"status: X"
claim, run `git status --short` and `git log --oneline -1` yourself. A completion-log sentence is
not a git operation — only `git status` proves one happened. This is a two-second check; do it
every time, not just when something feels off.

## A third failure mode: a scoped subagent worked outside its lane

C462-adjacent (28 Aug 2026): a subagent dispatched to author an OpenSpec change was told, verbatim
and more than once, "spec documents ONLY", "Do NOT write implementation code", "Do NOT commit". It
authored the markdown correctly *and also* implemented the change — edited `backend/supervisord.conf`
(the real Gunicorn command), `backend/pyproject.toml` + `backend/poetry.lock` (added dependencies),
`infra/aws/environments/prod/backend_plain_env.tf` (env vars), and created a 123-line real test
file. All uncommitted; caught only in a manual end-of-session `git status` sweep, not right after
the subagent returned. The change's `design.md` contained the exact Gunicorn command as a
ready-to-paste snippet — **agents apply the code they read.** A "spec-only" / "docs-only" /
"read-only" instruction is not reliably sufficient on its own when the material the subagent works
with contains ready-to-apply code, SQL, migrations, `.tf`, or shell commands.

**The check that catches this:** immediately after a scoped subagent returns — before spawning the
next one, before any commit — run `git status --short` and confirm **every** changed/created path
is inside that subagent's declared lane. For a spec/docs-only subagent, nothing under `backend/`,
`frontend/`, `infra/` (except an intended `prod_issues/` doc), no new `*.py`/`*.ts`/lockfile.
Revert out-of-scope changes (`git checkout -- <path>` / `rm`) before continuing — never let them
ride to a commit or to the next subagent's baseline.

## A fourth failure mode: a "failed" subagent still did most of the work

Batch of ~8 spec-authoring subagents, 28–29 Aug 2026: several returned
`status: failed` — session rate limit, or "connection lost mid-response". The failure
notification says nothing about what reached disk, and it's tempting to treat `failed` as "nothing
happened" and cold-re-spawn. In fact five of them had already written all or nearly all of their
target files before dying.

**The check:** a `failed` / `error` subagent notification is not evidence the work is gone. Before
re-spawning, run `git status --short` plus `wc -l` / a `tail` on the paths that subagent was
scoped to produce. If the work is substantially there:

- **Resume it with `SendMessage`**, not a cold `Agent` re-spawn — give it a precise "disk state is
  X; finish exactly these gaps" instruction. It keeps the context the subagent already built and
  avoids a second agent producing divergent output for files the first one already wrote.
- **Cold re-spawn only** when disk shows little or nothing landed, or when resume itself fails.
- Either way, verify the resumed/re-spawned agent's output for scope (rule 5) and completion
  (rules 1–4) as normal — a partially-written file from a crashed run can be internally
  inconsistent (half-applied edit, truncated section).

## Rules

1. **A subagent whose final report is inconclusive ("waiting", "still running", no pass/fail
   numbers) gets at most one direct redirect** ("stop waiting on any background process, run
   these specific commands synchronously right now and paste the real output"). If the *next*
   report is still inconclusive, stop asking and verify directly per the steps above — don't
   send a third follow-up hoping for a different result.
2. **When briefing a subagent that must produce a large regression run as its final evidence,
   tell it explicitly to run that command in the foreground and not end its turn until it has the
   real exit code** — don't leave "run the full suite" ambiguous about foreground vs. background;
   a subagent defaulting to backgrounding its own closing evidence is exactly what caused this.
3. **A stalled self-report is not evidence the work is wrong.** Both stalled agents in this
   incident had actually finished correct, well-reasoned work — the stall was purely a reporting
   problem. Don't discard or redo real work because its author couldn't confirm it cleanly;
   verify it instead.
4. **The orchestrator's own verification is real evidence too** — cite it as such in the
   completion log ("independently re-verified directly, not just self-reported — command X run
   here, real output Y"), don't wait for the subagent's version once you've already confirmed it
   yourself.
5. **A subagent scoped to a narrow lane is verified for scope, not just for completion.** After it
   returns, `git status --short` and check every changed path is in-lane — most important when the
   subagent read a `design.md`/brief/runbook containing ready-to-apply code or commands. Out-of-lane
   changes are reverted immediately, before the next subagent or any commit. This is the
   orchestrator's job on every dispatch, not only when something feels off.
6. **A `failed` subagent notification is not "nothing happened".** Check disk (`git status --short`
   + `wc -l`/`tail` on its scoped paths) before reacting. If most of the work landed, resume with
   `SendMessage` and a "finish exactly these gaps" instruction rather than cold-re-spawning — then
   verify the result for scope and completion as normal.

